{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 20, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "**A Whirlwind in Japan: Cherry Blossoms and Neon Dreams**\n\nSapporo’s crisp winter air greeted me first—steaming bowls of ramen curled around my fingers, snow dusting the rooftops of Susukino’s neon-lined streets. The contrast with Kyoto was sudden: ancient temples glowing under soft cherry blossoms, the scent of matcha drifting from teahouses where geisha glided past in practiced silence. I stood spellbound by Kinkaku-ji’s golden pavilion, its reflection shimmering on the pond, then got lost in the labyrinth of narrow alleys near Gion, where lanterns swayed and sake flowed freely.\n\nTokyo was a sensory overload after Kyoto’s tranquility. Shibuya’s scramble crossed overwhelmed me—hundreds of people moving as one, then stopping simultaneously at a flashing signal. I escaped to Akihabara for glowing anime posters and retro arcades, where salarymen played alongside cosplaying teens. A late-night bowl of gyudon from a 7-Eleven kept me going as I watched the Rainbow Bridge light up over Tokyo Bay.\n\nOsaka’s energy was unmatched. Dotonbori’s street food stalls beckoned with towering takoyaki and okonomiyaki sizzling under grills. The Universal Studios Japan park was a surreal mix of Studio Ghibli magic and Jurassic Park roars—my daughter’s laughter echoed louder than any monster’s.\n\nThe bullet train whisked me back to Tokyo, but the memories lingered: the quiet efficiency of bullet trains, the warmth of a tiny onsen shared with strangers, the bittersweet farewell at Narita Airport. Japan isn’t just a destination; it’s a feeling—where tradition meets the future in every cup of coffee, every subway ride, every fleeting moment.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.06550939381122589}, {"token": "A", "logprob": -0.30262133479118347}, {"token": " Wh", "logprob": -0.04418794438242912}, {"token": "irl", "logprob": -0.012623289600014687}, {"t